-------------------------------\n[2510.12196v1] GPU-Accelerated Algorithms for Process Mapping (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12196v1)\nciteturn2837view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12196v1\",\"lineno\":15}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 13 Mar 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:GPU-Accelerated Algorithms for Process Mapping\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Petr Samoldekin , cite7†Christian Schulz , cite8†Henning Woydt L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled GPU-Accelerated Algorithms for Process Mapping, by Petr Samoldekin and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Process mapping asks to assign vertices of a task graph to processing elements of a supercomputer such that the computational workload is balanced while the communication cost is minimized. Motivated by the recent success of GPU-based graph partitioners, we propose two GPU-accelerated algorithms for this optimization problem. The first algorithm employs hierarchical multisection, which partitions the task graph alongside the hierarchy of the supercomputer.\nL17: The method utilizes GPU-based graph partitioners to accelerate the mapping process. The second algorithm integrates process mapping directly into the modern multilevel graph partitioning pipeline. Vital phases like coarsening and refinement are accelerated by exploiting the parallelism of GPUs. In our experiments, both methods achieve speedups exceeding 300 when compared to state-of-the-art CPU-based algorithms.\nL18: The first algorithm has, on average, about 10 percent greater communication costs and thus remains competitive to CPU algorithms. The second approach is much faster, with a geometric mean speedup of 77.6 and peak speedup of 598 at the cost of lower solution quality. To our knowledge, these are the first GPU-based algorithms for process mapping.\nL19: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL20: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 8W10\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2510.12196 [cs.DC]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2510.12196v1 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12196†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Henning Martin Woydt [cite14†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Tue, 14 Oct 2025 06:42:20 UTC (146 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Fri, 13 Mar 2026 07:35:25 UTC (536 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled GPU-Accelerated Algorithms for Process Mapping, by Petr Samoldekin and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite15†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.DC\nL44: \nL45: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L46: \nL47: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-10 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite22†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic